---
/**
 * same-list.astro — **두 나라가 같은 주에 같은 한국 작품을 보나.**
 *
 * ── 🔴 왜 이 지면이 생겼나 (2026-09-03) ────────────────────────
 * `/all-ten` 을 만들다 걸린 것 — 2022-07-31 주에 인도네시아와 말레이시아의 시리즈 톱10이
 * «같은 열 편»이었다. 우리 기사가 그것을 「한 번 있었다」고만 적었다.
 * **얼마나 닮았나는 잰 적이 없었다.**
 *
 * ── ⛔ 이 지면이 지키는 것 — «작은 집합의 함정» ────────────────
 * 첫 판을 돌리니 위 열 쌍이 전부 유럽이었다(덴마크–노르웨이 97.1%).
 * 까닭은 그 나라들이 그 주에 한국 작품을 «한 편씩만» 받기 때문이다 — 1 대 1 이 같으면 100% 다.
 * ```
 *   모든 주 기준      덴마크–노르웨이 97.1%   적은 쪽 평균 1.1편   ← 잴 것이 적었다는 뜻
 *   다섯 편 이상 주만  말레이–싱가포르 77.9%   100주               ← 이쪽이 물음에 답한다
 * ```
 * ⛔ 그래서 **두 수를 나란히** 낸다. 큰 수만 내면 그 함정을 손님에게 넘기는 것이다.
 * ⛔ 「취향이 같다」고 안 쓴다 — 넷플릭스가 그 주 그 나라 목록에 올린 것이고, 그것은
 *   라이선스·공개일·차트 셈이 섞인 결과다.
 * ⛔ 수를 손으로 적지 않는다. `kcw-same-list.json` 에서 읽는다.
 */
import WikiTip from '../../layouts/WikiTip.astro';
import KcwDataBasis from '../../components/KcwDataBasis.astro';
import KcwRelatedArticles from '../../components/KcwRelatedArticles.astro';
import { 목록구조화 } from '../../lib/list-jsonld.ts';
import d from '../../data/kcw-same-list.json';

const 굵은쌍 = d.topThickPairs ?? [];
const 모든쌍 = d.topPairs ?? [];
const 한국굵 = d.koreaThick ?? [];
const 한국바닥 = d.koreaBottom ?? [];
const 수 = (n: number) => Number(n).toLocaleString('en-US');
const 으뜸 = 굵은쌍[0];

const TITLE = `Which two countries hold the same Korean Netflix list`;
const DESC = `${으뜸 ? `${으뜸.aName} and ${으뜸.bName} hold ${으뜸.thickOverlapPc}% of the same Korean titles in the same week` : 'Country pairs ranked by how much of the same Korean list they hold'}. `
  + `${수(d.pairsMeasured)} country pairs measured across ${d.weeksRead} weeks.`;
---

<WikiTip title={TITLE} description={DESC}
  canonical="https://www.kculturewire.com/same-list"
  jsonLd={목록구조화({
    주소: '/same-list',
    이름: 'Country pairs by shared Korean Netflix titles',
    설명: DESC,
    줄들: 굵은쌍.map((p: any) => ({ name: `${p.aName} and ${p.bName}` })),
  }, ['https://www.kculturewire.com/by-country'])}>
  <h1>Which two countries hold the same Korean list</h1>

  <p class="lead">
    Take one week and two countries. Of all the Korean titles either of them had on a Netflix top 10
    that week, what share did <b>both</b> have? We did that for every week and every pair &mdash;
    {' '}<b>{수(d.pairsMeasured)}</b> pairs across <b>{d.weeksRead}</b> weeks.
  </p>

  <section>
    <h2>Where both countries take several Korean titles a week</h2>
    <p>
      This is the table that answers the question. It uses only the weeks in which <b>both</b>
      {' '}countries had at least five Korean titles &mdash; see the next section for why that
      restriction is the whole measurement.
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Pair</th><th class="num">Same titles</th><th class="num">Weeks counted</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {굵은쌍.map((p: any) => (
          <tr>
            <td>{p.aName} &amp; {p.bName}</td>
            <td class="num"><b>{p.thickOverlapPc}%</b></td>
            <td class="num">{p.thickWeeks}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p class="note">
      Only <b>{d.thickPairsMeasured}</b> of the {수(d.pairsMeasured)} pairs have twenty or more such
      weeks. For every other pair we say <b>we could not measure it</b> rather than printing a
      number built on a handful of weeks.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>The trap, printed rather than hidden</h2>
    <p>
      Rank the same pairs using <b>every</b> week instead, and the table above disappears. These come
      out on top:
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Pair</th><th class="num">Same titles</th>
          <th class="num">Titles the smaller side had</th><th class="num">Weeks</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {모든쌍.slice(0, 8).map((p: any) => (
          <tr>
            <td>{p.aName} &amp; {p.bName}</td>
            <td class="num">{p.meanOverlapPc}%</td>
            <td class="num">{p.meanSmallerSet}</td>
            <td class="num">{p.weeks}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p class="note">
      Read the third column. In those weeks the smaller of the two countries had about
      {' '}<b>one</b> Korean title &mdash; and when both countries have one title and it is the same
      title, the overlap is 100%. <b>Agreement is cheap when there is almost nothing to agree
      about.</b> The high figures are a fact about how little Korean content those markets take, not
      about how alike they are.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Nowhere looks like Korea</h2>
    <p>
      Korea&rsquo;s own chart is the natural comparison, and it is the one that fails. Even on the
      restricted measure, the market whose Korean list most resembles Korea&rsquo;s own shares
      {' '}<b>{한국굵[0] ? `${한국굵[0].thickOverlapPc}%` : '—'}</b> of it.
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Paired with Korea</th><th class="num">Same titles</th><th class="num">Weeks</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {한국굵.map((p: any) => (
          <tr>
            <td>{p.a === 'KR' ? p.bName : p.aName}</td>
            <td class="num">{p.thickOverlapPc}%</td>
            <td class="num">{p.thickWeeks}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p class="note">
      At the other end, the lists that look least like Korea&rsquo;s are
      {' '}{한국바닥.slice(0, 3).map((p: any) => (p.a === 'KR' ? p.bName : p.aName)).join(', ')}
      {' '}&mdash; between {한국바닥[0]?.meanOverlapPc}% and {한국바닥[2]?.meanOverlapPc}% on the
      all-weeks measure. Those are markets where a single Korean title charts and it is rarely the
      one Korea is watching.
    </p>
  </section>

  <KcwDataBasis
    whatThisIs={d.unit}
    whatThisIsNot={d.whatThisIsNot}
    notes={[d.source, d.whyBothMustHaveOne]}
    counts={[
      { label: 'Chart rows read', value: d.rowsRead },
      { label: 'Weeks read', value: d.weeksRead },
      { label: 'Country pairs measured', value: d.pairsMeasured },
      { label: 'Pairs dropped for too few shared weeks', value: d.pairsDropped },
      { label: 'Pairs with 20+ weeks of five titles each', value: d.thickPairsMeasured },
    ]} />

  <p class="note">
    Each market&rsquo;s Korean share on its own is on <a href="/by-country">the country page</a>.
    {' '}The weeks a single chart went all the way &mdash; ten places out of ten &mdash; are on
    {' '}<a href="/all-ten">the all-ten page</a>.
  </p>

  <KcwRelatedArticles page="/same-list" />
</WikiTip>

<style>
  table { border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 0.8rem 0; }
  th, td { padding: 0.35rem 0.5rem; border-bottom: 1px solid #e5e7eb; text-align: left; }
  th.num, td.num { text-align: right; }
  .num { font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; }
  .note { font-size: 0.92em; color: #4b5563; }
</style>
